# Git commit co-author policy

Do not add `Co-authored-by` trailers to commit messages in this repository unless the user explicitly names a human co-author.

## Prohibited by default

- Never append Cursor's automatic trailer:
  - `Co-authored-by: Cursor <cursoragent@cursor.com>`
- Never add generic bot or agent co-authors (Cursor, Copilot, Claude, etc.).
- Do not add co-author lines "for attribution" when the agent helped write the change.

## When creating or amending commits

- Commit messages must contain only the subject, optional body, and nothing else unless the user requests a specific co-author.
- Before running `git commit` or `git commit --amend`, verify the message has no `Co-authored-by` lines.
- If a draft message includes a co-author trailer, remove it before committing.

## Why

GitHub counts co-authors as repository contributors. Unwanted trailers add bot accounts (for example, "cursor agent") to the public Contributors graph.

## Allowed exception

Add a `Co-authored-by` line only when the user explicitly provides a real person's name and email for shared authorship.
